    <h1>Pregnancy Weight Gain: What Is Healthy?</h1>

    <p>Weight gain is a normal and necessary part of pregnancy. The baby, placenta, amniotic fluid, increased blood volume, breast tissue, and maternal fat stores all contribute. But how much weight should you gain?</p>

    <h2>Weight Gain by Pre-Pregnancy BMI</h2>

    <p>The Institute of Medicine (IOM) and ACOG recommend the following weight gain ranges based on pre-pregnancy body mass index (BMI):</p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Pre-Pregnancy BMI</th>
          <th>Recommended Weight Gain</th>
          <th>Second/Third Trimester Rate</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Underweight (&lt; 18.5)</td>
          <td>28-40 pounds</td>
          <td>1-1.3 pounds/week</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Normal (18.5-24.9)</td>
          <td>25-35 pounds</td>
          <td>0.8-1 pound/week</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Overweight (25-29.9)</td>
          <td>15-25 pounds</td>
          <td>0.5-0.7 pounds/week</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Obese (30+)</td>
          <td>11-20 pounds</td>
          <td>0.4-0.6 pounds/week</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p><em>Source: Institute of Medicine, Weight Gain During Pregnancy: Reexamining the Guidelines; ACOG Committee Opinion 548.</em></p>

    <h2>Calorie Needs by Trimester</h2>

    <p>UK and US advice both reject the idea of eating for two, but they describe the extra energy you need in different ways.</p>

    <p>The NHS says you do not need to "eat for 2" even if you are expecting twins or triplets, and points to the Eatwell Guide for balance over a week rather than at every meal. The Scientific Advisory Committee on Nutrition (SACN) states that in the last three months of pregnancy women need around an extra 200 kilocalories a day above their usual requirements. Women who are exclusively breastfeeding need around an extra 330 kilocalories a day for the first six months after birth.</p>

    <p>ACOG frames the same point as quality rather than quantity: aim to eat twice as healthy, not twice as much.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Have a healthy diet in pregnancy, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/keeping-well/have-a-healthy-diet/; SACN, Nutrition and maternal weight outcomes report summary, https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/nutrition-and-maternal-weight-outcomes-sacn-report/nutrition-and-maternal-weight-outcomes-sacn-report-summary; ACOG, Healthy Eating During Pregnancy, https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/healthy-eating-during-pregnancy</em></p>

    <AdUnit slot="925728676" format="auto" />
    <h2>Where Does the Weight Go?</h2>

    <p>In a typical pregnancy, weight gain is distributed roughly as follows:</p>


    <ul>
      <li>Baby: 7-8 pounds</li>
      <li>Placenta: 1-2 pounds</li>
      <li>Amniotic fluid: 2 pounds</li>
      <li>Breast tissue: 1-3 pounds</li>
      <li>Increased blood volume: 3-4 pounds</li>
      <li>Increased fluid volume: 2-3 pounds</li>
      <li>Uterus: 2 pounds</li>
      <li>Maternal fat stores: 6-8 pounds</li>
    </ul>

