    <h2>Where the Guidelines Disagree</h2>

    <p>Not every authority uses a target range. This matters if you are trying to work out which number applies to you.</p>

    <p>United States. ACOG Committee Opinion 548, published in January 2013, adopts the Institute of Medicine 2009 ranges. It tells clinicians to work out a woman's BMI at the first prenatal visit and counsel her on the benefits of appropriate weight gain, nutrition, and exercise. The committee also records that some physicians argue the targets are too high for overweight and obese women, and that the ranges do not separate degrees of obesity.</p>

    <p>United Kingdom. NICE guideline NG247, published and last reviewed on 15 January 2025, does not set a total weight gain target. It covers nutrition and weight management in pregnancy, but gives no detailed advice on what makes up a healthy diet, and it refers weight management before and after pregnancy to the separate NICE guideline NG246. Recommendation 1.2.14 lists the risks of gaining too much and too little weight and points people to weighing equipment.</p>

    <p>UK expert committee. SACN, reporting in February 2026, states there are currently no UK guidelines for "appropriate" gestational weight gain. It adds that pre-pregnancy BMI is likely to have a greater influence on the health of the mother and baby than the amount of weight gained during pregnancy. Where people want to monitor change, NICE NG247 suggests they can refer to the US National Academy of Medicine guidelines, formerly the IOM ranges.</p>

    <p>Global. The World Health Organization established a Technical Advisory Group on Gestational Weight Gain (TAG-GWG) in May 2023. Its role is to advise on developing global gestational weight gain standards and optimal ranges, because WHO describes such standards as a critical gap for maternal and child health. No global range exists yet.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: ACOG Committee Opinion 548, https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2013/01/weight-gain-during-pregnancy; NICE NG247, https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng247; SACN, Nutrition and maternal weight outcomes report summary, https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/nutrition-and-maternal-weight-outcomes-sacn-report/nutrition-and-maternal-weight-outcomes-sacn-report-summary; WHO Technical Advisory Group on Gestational Weight Gain, https://www.who.int/groups/technical-advisory-group-on-gestational-weight-gain-(tag-gwg)</em></p>

    <h2>Why the 2009 Ranges Are Contested</h2>

    <p>The IOM 2009 ranges were not built on new cohort data. A review in Obesity Reviews notes that the 2009 guidelines varied only minimally from the 1990 guidelines, that the 1990 recommendations rested on data from the 1980s, and that many critics advocate lower targets, especially for women with obesity.</p>

    <p>The IOM committee itself said its guidelines are intended for use among women in the United States, may apply to women in other developed countries, but are not intended for areas where women are substantially shorter or thinner than American women.</p>

    <p>A separate perspective in Obesity argues the ranges are hard to apply at the bedside. Estimates of weight at conception and of total gain vary by method, and small differences can move a woman into a different BMI category and a different recommendation.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: Weight gain in pregnancy: is less truly more for mother and infant? Obesity Reviews, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4989622/; Institute of Medicine, Weight Gain During Pregnancy: Reexamining the Guidelines, chapter 7, https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/12584/chapter/9; Weight gain in pregnancy and application of the 2009 IOM guidelines, Obesity, https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/oby.20951</em></p>

    <h2>Weight Gain by Trimester</h2>

    <h3>First Trimester (Weeks 1-13)</h3>
    <p>Most women gain 1-5 pounds total in the first trimester. If you have significant nausea or vomiting, you may lose weight initially. This is usually not a concern if you are able to stay hydrated and eat some calories.</p>

    <h3>Second Trimester (Weeks 14-27)</h3>
    <p>Weight gain typically accelerates in the second trimester as nausea fades and appetite returns. Most women gain about 1 pound per week.</p>

    <h3>Third Trimester (Weeks 28-40)</h3>
    <p>Weight gain continues at about 1 pound per week, though some women gain more in the final weeks. Growth may slow or plateau in the last 2-3 weeks.</p>

