    <h2>Healthy Weight Gain Tips</h2>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Eat nutrient-dense foods:</strong> Choose foods rich in vitamins, minerals, and protein over empty calories.</li>
      <li><strong>Stay hydrated:</strong> Drink plenty of water. Thirst is sometimes mistaken for hunger. ACOG suggests 8 to 12 cups a day during pregnancy.</li>
      <li><strong>Exercise regularly:</strong> Moderate exercise during pregnancy is safe for most women and can help manage weight gain.</li>
      <li><strong>Listen to your body:</strong> Eat when you are hungry and stop when you are full.</li>
      <li><strong>Avoid "eating for two":</strong> NHS advice is that you do not need to "eat for 2", even if you are expecting twins or triplets. SACN puts the extra need at around 200 kilocalories a day in the last three months of pregnancy.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>If You Are Above or Below the Range</h2>

    <p>Gaining outside a published range is common, and it is not a verdict on you or on your baby.</p>

    <p>Above the range. NICE NG247 names the risks of gaining too much weight, including having a baby who is large for gestational age, developing hypertension or gestational diabetes, and needing a caesarean section. The guideline says advice should cover monitoring diet and physical activity and where to find free or low-cost support.</p>

    <p>Below the range. NICE NG247 names the risk of gaining too little weight, regardless of pre-pregnancy BMI, including having a baby who is small for gestational age. ACOG Committee Opinion 548 links inadequate weight gain with decreased birth weight.</p>

    <p>Intentional weight loss in pregnancy is not recommended. SACN states this directly, because of potential adverse effects on the baby.</p>

    <p>If your gain is outside the range that applies to you, take the numbers to your own clinician. They can weigh your pre-pregnancy BMI, your baby's growth, and any condition such as gestational diabetes, and set a plan for you. This guide cannot do that for you.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NICE NG247, https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng247; ACOG Committee Opinion 548, https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2013/01/weight-gain-during-pregnancy; SACN, Nutrition and maternal weight outcomes report summary, https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/nutrition-and-maternal-weight-outcomes-sacn-report/nutrition-and-maternal-weight-outcomes-sacn-report-summary</em></p>

    <h2>When to Call Your Clinician</h2>

    <p>Contact your healthcare provider if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You are losing weight in the first trimester (beyond normal nausea)</li>
      <li>You are gaining weight too quickly or too slowly</li>
      <li>You have concerns about your weight or nutrition</li>
      <li>You have a history of an eating disorder</li>
    </ul>

